{"title":"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find all integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/1/8f1e2533d6abdb11a9a1626c0240fab1a9200fc6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> such that among any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> positive real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/9/5e97a8af68fbc8e357d3ee0eba452022b06c1875.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/6/64683f315744c851898c8d65dace1a8aa31b93ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dots$\" width=\"19\" height=\"2\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> with\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/e/ceee7231e7bcd541488519c12e11c9ff7478d0c3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\max(a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n) \\le n \\cdot \\min(a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n),\\]\" width=\"346\" height=\"18\" />\nthere exist three that are the side lengths of an acute triangle.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Without loss of generality, assume that the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/c/6bca7f68c5ef65ce19007f70e9fcc46262f6f81f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{a\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" /> is ordered from least to greatest so that the bounding condition becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/302b515068a7a7611200670d8545ebef9e745e0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n \\le n \\cdot a_1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"87\" height=\"14\" /> Now set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/5/705a8dbf3e8a17f95df2f7988c34311bc1f43e89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_i \\equiv \\frac{a_i}{a_1},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"62\" height=\"35\" /> and since a triangle with sidelengths from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/c/6bca7f68c5ef65ce19007f70e9fcc46262f6f81f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{a\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" /> will be similar to the corresponding triangle from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/8/d386aa6a97c68192a0cb7afc24c5895098fbed3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{b\\},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> we simply have to show the existence of acute triangles in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/1/fd1d7a26ecc21e12e82a24ce6d7d6953270fbd86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{b\\}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"29\" height=\"18\" /> Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/7/ad79bea94962f7b6a51157fd8465a31a32eb4b7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_1 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> and for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/7/b07079c932de0bc113ed9dfdea6b74ee98468f8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_i \\le n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>Now three arbitrary sidelengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/a/afabf3baf7da727ad7caeebd011dfda28655e9a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\le y \\le z,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"17\" /> will form a valid triangle if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/8/28846cf8a84a843a58c855b11d23661efa5eb298.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y&gt;z.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"14\" /> Furthermore, this triangle will be acute if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/9/4496e762b89f22c4a0476b60931a96ef6102afb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + y^2 &gt; z^2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"101\" height=\"18\" /> However, the first inequality can actually be inferred from the second, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d27cca8d8ac0fa03faefa3cb768102ee42b5374c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y&gt;z \\longleftrightarrow x^2 + y^2 +2xy &gt; z^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"270\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/a/abaf1f4bdf803c0f6f809aef04357676a307fcee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"31\" height=\"17\" /> is trivially greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/4/604c735470dd0152ca1cff75fe26eadf5cef7f49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> So we just need to find all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that there is necessarily a triplet of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />'s for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/2/112b65a76c7d7bf13c8e1400b6cb6876411cc212.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_i^2 + b_j^2 &gt; b_k^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"92\" height=\"21\" /> (where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/6/0a62bc7678d4a816a3da85fd1ac04a60cb8aca4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_i &lt; b_j &lt; b_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"17\" />).\n</p><p>We now make another substitution: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/b/21b9c7183024724b97b5e1001f4f48274f8fbe26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_i \\equiv b_i ^2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"19\" /> So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/0/e50622a437ed869fea40aa757e2fea9fca7cf8c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_1 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" /> and for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/2/89224e293bacad0586b996cae76db7762d25939a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_i \\le n^2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"17\" /> Now we examine the smallest possible sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/0/220b9c4db0d08d29645e3cd318add4e2774df7b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{c\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"25\" height=\"18\" /> for small <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> for which the conditions of the problem are not met. Note that by smallest, we mean the set whose greatest element is as small as possible. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/5635737307f5f0651cced8ee2e6558a426fd27b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, then the smallest possible set, call it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/8/b1832582f84494f15b8ced2a65366751bb5ec1fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{s_3\\},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> is trivially <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/5/f8588d2150e05b6f4f7ae352f747cdffa0716ec4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,1,2\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" />, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/00322295674c54c5413520f7493470ff32ae3cec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d4142d351ef99cc26a94e44c1912d904dd4a0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> are obviously minimized and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/a/94aaab012b431d7695fa1ae72d7a88b05baf09f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> follows as minimal. Using this as the base case, we see inductively that in general <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/4/9748dc720781446a27db08fa172aedfa2996a150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{s_n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> is the set of the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> Fibonacci numbers. To show this note that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/e/ece550be1ab1d08d320d60f590021c6b00f3c111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{s_n\\} = \\{F_0, F_1, ... F_n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"167\" height=\"18\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e0bffbc9ddcf1b80ce7dfb44e68e624536b8cb2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{s_{n+1}\\} = \\{F_0, F_1, ... F_n, c_{n+1}\\}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"230\" height=\"18\" /> The smallest possible value for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/3/62387fa06875f556c3e32bcbc7b69196e8df1079.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"31\" height=\"12\" /> is the sum of the two greatest values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/4/9748dc720781446a27db08fa172aedfa2996a150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{s_n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> which are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/c/84c65efa092f022b2e04b08f5b95b20d015f6f88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/4/b842b4552478b69732a9aea702309d8c51e43056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />. But these sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/7/fc7b2937746dafabc99ef8ba0e4951bcf9653876.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc618bdd367e8b169110a6dbf1330402fabdfb0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{s_{n+1}\\} = \\{F_0, F_1, ... F_{n+1}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"201\" height=\"18\" /> and our induction is complete.\n</p><p>Now since we know that the Fibonacci set is the smallest possible set which does not satisfy the conditions of the problem, then any set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/0/220b9c4db0d08d29645e3cd318add4e2774df7b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{c\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"25\" height=\"18\" /> whose greatest term is less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/c/84c65efa092f022b2e04b08f5b95b20d015f6f88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> must satisfy the conditions. And since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/0/220b9c4db0d08d29645e3cd318add4e2774df7b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{c\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"25\" height=\"18\" /> is bounded between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/0/6006bba637eef443381fbaff768fa62d9ff41e62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"17\" />, then the conditions of the problem are met if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299401493bca890a6520eb8646a4f9c0d91db3d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_{n-1} &gt; n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"17\" />. The first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> for which this restriction is satisfied is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/8/608f4cbd198d2af6bf20b99fc5e126469e09ec84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=13$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> and the exponential behavior of the Fibonacci numbers ensure that every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030e19e4e2f1c074127cb50581e8496470c5419f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> will also satisfy this restriction. So the final solution set is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/8/aa8fc0ecaf537c69fbcb4b8c7c441ed76a4cb125.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\{n \\ge 13\\}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"85\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Outline:</b>\n</p><p>1. Define the Fibonacci numbers to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/f/eef36d8b3c71cd00802eb6c16c99f2b131d5e28f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_1 = F_2 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"95\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/3/b33c3ab8b09fda85c1b9ac2ec37db52181ca83bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_k = F_{k-1} + F_{k-2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"138\" height=\"15\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5bb76208dae30e44513fa10ff49ba1329094713.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\ge 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>2. If the chosen <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7540eb95af263b56e1c224f32ef53782ba3ea371.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_n \\le n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" />, then choose the sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/7/927eacdd8347211d8cb67a34b7a2b35b64c1b237.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_k = \\sqrt{F_k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"22\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/0/810bc3b19926c7e47659a31b7a3e7d177f1fd281.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\le k \\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"15\" />. It is easy to verify that such a sequence satisfies the condition that the largest term is less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> times the smallest term. Also, because for any three terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/2/7b2f246ea8a715cd61a0e42551029abbe2aa8ab6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\sqrt{F_a}, y = \\sqrt{F_b}, z = \\sqrt{F_c}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"228\" height=\"22\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/a/5ba70df5a2f9630194f32f50010cd2e78318c98d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;b&lt;c$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/f/bbfc01536374763768d8ff66166958e38ffac87f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + y^2 = F_a + F_b \\le F_{b-1} + F_b = F_{b+1} \\le F_c = z^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"383\" height=\"19\" />, x, y, z do not form an acute triangle. Thus, all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7540eb95af263b56e1c224f32ef53782ba3ea371.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_n \\le n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" /> do not work.\n</p><p>3. It is easy to observe via a contradiction argument that all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/0/670e9d2a3100c5032598c665a0d1031a8f7075c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_n &gt; n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" /> produce an acute triangle. (If, without loss of generality, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> is an increasing sequence, such that no three (in particular, consecutive) terms form an acute triangle, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/7/c17c2a8a00cc1102794c55c5cbaabb84854bc500.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_2^2 \\ge F_1a_1^2, a_3^2 \\ge a_2^2 + a_1^2 \\ge F_2a^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"242\" height=\"19\" />, and by induction <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/e/b2e55d15c11fa372b6eeb91462d9c339c30cb1ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n^2 &gt; F_na_1^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"19\" />, a contradiction to the condition's inequality.)\n</p><p>4. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/c/72c8ffc8c985ac6d09d29b99cf2904b7e1dba532.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_{12} = 144 = 12^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/1/f41714b303b358b71cafbf09904f922a2af3d301.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_{13} = 233 &gt; 169 = 13^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"177\" height=\"17\" />. It is easily to verify through strong induction that all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> greater than 12 make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/0/670e9d2a3100c5032598c665a0d1031a8f7075c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_n &gt; n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/2/2d2117c71802d5b39192c1a4d9e762a296f26e0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{n \\ge 13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"65\" height=\"25\" /> is the desired solution set.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3\">Video Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>See the solution in action! \n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/yqY910bE02o\">https://youtu.be/yqY910bE02o</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\">USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAJMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAJMO Problems and Solutions\">USAJMO Problems and Solutions</a></li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO\" title=\"2012 USAMO\">2012 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2012\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\"><b>First Problem</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAJMO\" title=\"2012 USAJMO\">2012 USAJMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAJMO_Problems\" title=\"2012 USAJMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=176&amp;year=2012\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2012 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2012 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\"